Hier ist die aktualisierte und um einen leicht verständlichen Leitfaden für das Menüband (Ribbon) ergänzte Version des Handbuchs:

---

# Mailsorter - User Manual

This manual guides you through installing, importing, and using **Mailsorter** to manage your Microsoft Outlook folders and file emails with lightning speed.

---

## 1. Importing the VBA Files & Adding a Ribbon Button

Before you can run the tool, you need to import the source files and create an easy way to click it from your Outlook menu:

### Step A: Import the Files

1. Open Microsoft Outlook.
2. Press `Alt + F11` to open the Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) Editor.
3. In the VBA menu bar, go to **File** -> **Import File...** (`Ctrl + M`).
4. Navigate to the `/src` folder of this repository and select all required source and form files (`.bas`, `.frm`, and `.frx`).
5. Ensure the **TreeView Reference** is active:
* Go to **Tools** -> **References** in the VBA menu.
* Make sure **Microsoft Windows Common Controls 6.0 (SP6)** is checked. If it is missing or grayed out, consult your system administrator.


6. Close the VBA editor.

### Step B: Add a Button to the Outlook Ribbon (Toolbar)

To run your new macro with a single click without opening the VBA editor every time, follow these beginner-friendly steps to add it to your Outlook ribbon:

1. Open Microsoft Outlook and go to **File** in the top left corner.
2. Click on **Options** at the very bottom of the left menu. A new window will open.
3. In the Outlook Options window, click on **Customize Ribbon** on the left side.
4. Look at the right side of the window (where your menu tabs like "Home" or "Send / Receive" are listed).
* Select the tab where you want the button to appear (for example, click on **Home**).
* Click the **New Group** button at the bottom right to create a custom section inside that tab (you can right-click the new group afterwards to **Rename** it to something like *Mailsorter*).


5. Now look at the top left drop-down menu that says *Choose commands from:* and change it from "Popular Commands" to **Macros**.
6. You should now see your imported macro (`modEmailSorter.RunFolderPicker`) listed in that box.
7. Click on your macro name to select it, then click the **Add >>** button in the middle to move it into your newly created custom group on the right side.
8. *(Optional)* You can click **Rename** on your added macro on the right side to give it a nice display name (e.g., `Sort Emails`) and pick a nice icon for it.
9. Click **OK** at the bottom right to save your changes.

You will now see your brand-new button directly in your Outlook ribbon and can launch the tool with a single click!

---

## 2. Modes of Operation

The tool operates in two distinct modes depending on whether you have selected emails in Outlook:

* **Move / Copy Mode (Active when emails are selected):**
If you select one or more emails in your Outlook message list and trigger the macro, the main form opens in filing mode. Double-clicking a folder or using the action buttons will instantly move or copy the selected items to that destination.
* **Navigation / "Go To Folder" Mode (Active when NO emails are selected):**
If you run the macro without selecting any emails, the tool opens as a rapid folder launcher. Double-clicking a folder instantly navigates your Outlook main window to that directory.

---

## 3. Form Guide: How Every Window & Control Works

### A. The Main Folder Picker Form (`frmFolderPicker`)

This is your central hub for finding and selecting folders instantly.

* **Live Search Filter Box:**
* *What it does:* Type any part of a destination folder name here and press `Enter` (or watch it filter dynamically).
* *Why it helps:* It cuts through deep, nested directory trees in milliseconds following the *"Find, Don't Search"* philosophy.


* **Folder Tree View:**
* *What it does:* Displays your active mailboxes and folder hierarchies visually.
* *Interaction:* Expand/collapse nodes manually or use the search filter to locate specific targets. Double-clicking a folder triggers the primary action (Move/Copy or Go To).


* **Recent Folders History Panel:**
* *What it does:* Tracks your last 20 visited destination folders automatically.
* *Why it helps:* Frequent filing targets are kept right at your fingertips, allowing you to route subsequent emails with a single click.


* **Action Buttons:**
* **Move:** Relocates the selected email(s) to the highlighted destination folder.
* **Copy:** Duplicates the selected email(s) into the target folder while keeping the original.
* **New Subfolder:** Creates a brand-new subfolder on the fly directly inside the currently selected directory.
* **Settings:** Opens the configuration window (`frmSettings`).
* **Cancel / Close:** Closes the form without taking any action.



---

### B. The Settings Form (`frmSettings`)

Accessible via the settings button on the main form, this window allows you to customize how mailboxes and folders behave.

* **Listbox: Mailboxes & Stores**
* *What it does:* Displays all detected email accounts and stores available in your Outlook profile (including shared mailboxes and archives).


* **Checkbox: Include in folder search / Active account**
* *What it does:* Determines whether a specific mailbox or store is actively scanned and included when you use the live folder search. Unchecking a store keeps its irrelevant folders hidden from your daily workflow.


* **Listbox: Folder Structure / Roots**
* *What it does:* Shows the primary folder hierarchy for a selected store, letting you define specific root boundaries if needed.


* **Action Buttons:**
* **Save Changes:** Commits your updated configurations, updates the folder cache, and closes the settings window.
* **Cancel:** Discards any unedited configuration adjustments and closes the window.
